Alright, let’s break **Non-Recursive DFS** into the simplest explanation possible.

You already know DFS means:
> Go **deep** as much as possible, then **backtrack**.

When using **recursion**, the system automatically handles backtracking using its **call stack**.

When using **non-recursive DFS**, **we must use our own stack**.

---

## 🚀 Non-Recursive DFS (Concept)

We do 4 basic steps repeatedly:

1️⃣ Visit the current vertex  
2️⃣ Mark it visited  
3️⃣ Push it to stack  
4️⃣ Move to any **unvisited** adjacent vertex  
→ If **none found**, **pop** from stack (go back)

Repeat until stack becomes empty.

That’s it. Nothing more.

---

## 🔥 Quick Algorithm — Easy to remember

```
1. Push start vertex into stack
2. Mark start as visited and print it

3. While stack not empty:
      Look for an unvisited adjacent vertex
        → If found: visit, mark, push
        → If not found: pop (go back)
```

---

## 🧠 How to Think During Execution

Imagine you are walking in a maze:

✔ Every room = Vertex  
✔ Every door = Edge  
✔ Every time you enter a room, put a **stone** (push)  
✔ When stuck → pick last stone and go back (pop)

That’s DFS non-recursive!

---

## 🧩 Small Dry-Run Example (Same as your image)

Start from **V1**

| Step | Action | Stack | Visited |
|------|--------|-------|---------|
| 1 | Visit V1, push | V1 | V1 |
| 2 | Go to V2 | V1,V2 | V1,V2 |
| 3 | Go to V4 | V1,V2,V4 | V1,V2,V4 |
| 4 | Go to V8 | V1,V2,V4,V8 | V1,V2,V4,V8 |
| 5 | Go to V5 | V1,V2,V4,V8,V5 | V1,V2,V4,V8,V5 |
| 6 | From V5 – no unvisited → POP | V1,V2,V4,V8 | same |
| 7 | From V8 – go to V6 | V1,V2,V4,V8,V6 | add V6 |
| 8 | From V6 – go to V3 | add V3 | add V3 |
| 9 | V3 → V7 | add V7 | add V7 |
| 10 | V7 → none → POP repeatedly until stack empty |

Final DFS:
```
V1, V2, V4, V8, V5, V6, V3, V7
```

